<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Q'umarkaj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Utatlán</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Q'umarkaj, called Utatlán by the Spanish, was the capital of the K'iche' Maya kingdom in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, near modern Santa Cruz del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiche-people">Quiché</a>. Founded in the 15th century on a plateau protected by ravines, it held temples, palaces and a ballcourt. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> burned it in 1524 after executing its kings, and its ruins are still used for Maya ceremonies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qumarkaj.jpg" alt="Q'umarkaj" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Jones from Kelowna, BC, Canada · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%27umarcaaj_(Former_K%27iche%27_Capital)_-_Outside_Santa_Cruz_del_Quiche_-_Quiche_-_Guatemala_-_03_(15940257161).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
